The `Motor` base class this Module implements comes from a separate product,
https://github.com/starpang888/Motor, itself derived from
https://github.com/QDU-Robomaster/Motor (Apache-2.0).

Modifications from the upstream version:

- Declared `starpang888/Motor` instead of `QDU-Robomaster/Motor` as the
  manifest dependency, and changed the Module id in the README from
  `QDU-Robomaster/RMMotor` to `starpang888/RMMotor`.
- Removed the macros `M3508_M2006_ID_SETTING_ID` and `M3508_MAX_ABS_LSB`,
  which upstream defines but never uses, and turned the remaining numeric
  macros into `constexpr` constants.
- Replaced the unused `ramfs.hpp` and `thread.hpp` includes with the directly
  used `libxr_mem.hpp`. Upstream reaches `LibXR::Memory::FastCopy()` only
  through the `libxr.hpp` umbrella header that `can.hpp` pulls in.
- Extracted the model and feedback ID mapping into the `constexpr` functions
  `DeriveConfig()` and `DerivePlacement()`, and the per-model torque constant,
  maximum current and full-scale control value into the `constexpr` functions
  `KGetTorque()`, `GetCurrentMAX()` and `GetLSB()`. These are now public and can
  be evaluated at compile time; upstream computes them in the constructor with
  two `switch` statements. The derived values are unchanged.
- Made an unsupported model and feedback ID combination inert. Upstream falls through to
  group index 0 and slot 0, registers a callback for CAN ID 0 and joins group 0, so a
  mistyped `feedback_id` silently takes over the slot of the motor that belongs there and
  the group then flushes frames with control frame ID 0. Such an instance now logs an
  error, registers nothing, joins no group and sends nothing.
- Added a stall guard to `PackAndSend()`. Upstream sends a group frame only when
  every constructed member of the group has written a command in the current
  round, so a single member that is constructed but never controlled leaves
  `pending_mask` permanently below `group_mask`, and the whole group silently
  stops receiving commands. The frame is now flushed with the current buffer
  once `STALL_FLUSH_MS` (20 ms) have passed without a send, and a warning is
  logged once per stall; slots never written keep the 0-current value they get
  when the group is created.
- Anchored the stall guard's wait timer to the moment the group is created
  rather than leaving it at 0, so the first round after construction is not
  flushed prematurely.
